DurableStorage	native	topic	the message is published with the AMQP header's `durable` flag set, and a durable queue writes it to disk; what the queue is was decided when it was declared, which on this profile is not this driver's doing
AtMostOnce	unsupported	none	this driver attaches every receiving link unsettled and settles each delivery explicitly, so a delivery is redelivered rather than lost; a pre-settled receiving link would be at-most-once and there is no configuration for one
AtLeastOnce	native	group	a transfer is unsettled until this consumer's disposition, and detaching the link returns everything unsettled on it to the queue, where RabbitMQ hands it to the next consumer; DeliveryInfo.Attempt is the AMQP header's `delivery-count` plus one, which is a protocol field here rather than RabbitMQ's x-delivery-count header extension
ExactlyOnceDelivery	unsupported	none	
ExplicitAck	native	group	Ack is the `accepted` disposition for the delivery, which is final when the frame is written under the default receiver settle mode `first` and confirmed by the broker under `second`
Nack	native	group	a requeue is `released`, which does NOT count the attempt; a drop and a dead-letter are both `rejected`, which on a queue with a dead-letter exchange routes there rather than destroying the message -- AMQP 1.0 has no destroy outcome, and that is the safe direction stated rather than hidden
DelayedRedelivery	unsupported	none	a `modified` disposition returns the delivery to the queue at once, so there is nothing to hold it back. The other profile builds a delay from a TTL queue that dead-letters back or from the rabbitmq_delayed_message_exchange plugin; the first needs topology this profile cannot create, and how a delay reaches the plugin over AMQP 1.0 is not verified here, so NackOptions.Delay is refused with ErrUnsupported and settles nothing
ScheduledPublish	unsupported	none	RabbitMQ has no AMQP 1.0 spelling of a delivery delay. The rabbitmq_delayed_message_exchange plugin reads an `x-delay` HEADER on AMQP 0-9-1, and which AMQP 1.0 section and key that becomes -- a message annotation, an application property, neither -- is not verified here, so PublishOptions.Delay and DeliverAt are refused before any I/O rather than written where they may be ignored
MessageTTL	native	topic	PublishOptions.TTL travels as the AMQP message header's `ttl` in milliseconds, which RabbitMQ reads as the message's own expiry; whether the expiry survives a dead-letter hop into `<queue>.dlq`, and whether the AMQP 1.0 view carries the `x-death` record of one, is not verified here
Priority	conditional	group	the subscription's queue was declared with x-max-priority, so a priority above the ceiling is clamped by the broker; RabbitMQ refuses x-max-priority on a quorum queue, so a prioritised subscription is a classic queue and the delivery attempt count goes with it
DeadLetter	native	group	a `rejected` disposition routes the message through the queue's own dead-letter exchange to `<queue>.dlx` and `<queue>.dlq`, which whoever declared the queue had to set: this profile declares nothing and publishes no copy of its own
ConsumerGroups	native	group	several receiving links on one queue compete: the broker hands each message to one of them, bounded by each link's own credit
DurableSubscriptions	native	group	a subscription is a durable queue, which keeps accumulating while no link is attached; detaching a link is not unsubscribing
FanOut	native	topic	one queue per subscription, each bound to the topic's exchange, so two subscriptions each receive every message; the bindings are whoever declared the topology's, because this profile creates none
Replay	unsupported	none	a receiving link attaches to an address and reads what is there; AMQP 1.0 has no position, so there is nothing to rewind
SeekByTime	unsupported	none	
SeekBySequence	unsupported	none	
OrderedByKey	unsupported	none	a sending link's address carries the routing key (`/exchanges/<exchange>/<routing-key>`), so the key cannot be the routing key per message the way WithKeyAsRoutingKey makes it on AMQP 0-9-1; with the key in an application property the broker does not read it
OrderedByPartition	native	group	a queue is FIFO, so one queue's messages are delivered in the order the exchange routed them while a single consumer settles them in order; a requeue puts a message back at the head and a second consumer breaks it, which is the specification's own caveat and not this driver's
OrderedGlobally	unsupported	none	
NativeHeaders	native	topic	a portable header is an AMQP application property, which is the section RabbitMQ converts to and from the AMQP 0-9-1 field table -- so a header published through one RabbitMQ profile reads back through the other. A key longer than 255 bytes is legal here and cannot become a field-table key, which is an interoperability ceiling rather than a limit this driver enforces
BinaryHeaders	native	topic	an application-property value is typed, so a header value that is not valid UTF-8 travels as an AMQP binary and comes back byte for byte rather than as a byte array inside a field table
DuplicateHeaders	unsupported	none	application-properties is a map, so one key cannot carry two values; MetadataPortableEnvelope and MetadataAdaptive carry a repeated key in the envelope instead
BatchPublish	emulatedvolatile	topic	AMQP 1.0 has no batched transfer: the core publishes every message of the batch and this driver awaits each disposition, so a batch is NOT atomic -- a failure part-way through leaves the messages before it published
AtomicBatchPublish	unsupported	none	
BatchReceive	native	group	the link's credit lets the broker send several transfers before any is settled, which is what a batched fetch reads; credit is the AMQP 1.0 answer to `basic.qos` and is set by Config.Credit
BatchSettle	unsupported	none	a disposition frame can cover a range of delivery ids and go-amqp exposes only the per-message wrappers, so a batched settlement here would be a loop
ExtendLease	unsupported	none	AMQP 1.0 has no lease: an unsettled delivery is this receiver's for as long as the link lives, so there is no deadline to extend and DeliveryInfo.LeaseExpires is the zero time
IdempotentPublish	unsupported	none	
Deduplication	unsupported	none	
Transactions	unsupported	none	
ServerFilter	unsupported	none	a receiving link's source address names a queue, so the only filtering the portable API can express is which queue is read. RabbitMQ 4.1 added AMQP filter expressions for streams; this driver does not set one and nothing here has verified them, so the claim is not made
WildcardSubscribe	unsupported	none	a wildcard is a binding pattern on a topic exchange. A receiving link addresses a queue (`/queues/<queue>`), so a pattern is not expressible in the portable Topic, and the binding that would carry it is topology this profile does not create
Admin	unsupported	none	AMQP 1.0 has no topology API. RabbitMQ 4 has an AMQP 1.0 management extension over a `/management` node and this profile does not implement it, so Client.Admin answers ErrUnsupported; declare topology with drivers/rabbitmq, the HTTP management API or rabbitmqctl
RequestReply	unsupported	none	direct reply-to (`amq.rabbitmq.reply-to`) is a RabbitMQ extension of AMQP 0-9-1 and has no AMQP 1.0 form. A reply address still travels in the `reply-to` property, which is the portable emulation the core provides rather than a native mechanism
PublisherConfirm	native	topic	the peer's `accepted` disposition for the transfer, which ConfirmAccepted waits for. There is no confirm MODE to enable and no cumulative form: every transfer on an unsettled link gets its own disposition, so the per-message, windowed and asynchronous strategies have no equivalent and what a caller sizes is the credit window
DurableConfirm	conditional	topic	the peer's `accepted` disposition for a transfer on an unsettled link, which RabbitMQ returns for a durable quorum queue once the message has been written to a majority of the queue's replicas. There is no confirm MODE to select and no cumulative form: every transfer gets its own disposition, so the three strategies have no equivalent here
NativeTracing	unsupported	none	
Receipts	conditional	topic	the receipt carries the confirmation boundary and the time the disposition arrived; AMQP 1.0's disposition assigns no identifier, so PublishReceipt.MessageID stays empty and Partition, Offset and Sequence stay zero
limit	MaxMessageBytes	unknown
limit	MaxBatchMessages	unknown
limit	MaxBatchBytes	unknown
limit	MaxHeaderCount	unknown
limit	MaxHeaderBytes	255
limit	MaxDelay	unknown
limit	MaxLease	unknown
